        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "ad471e51-22d3-5c17-91e3-4ab85dd9c402",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jacobus Arminius, Theodore Beza, John Calvin, Geert Booij\n- Organizations: Theological University of Geneva, University of Leiden\n- Concepts: Arminianism, Calvinist–Arminian debate, Protestant Reformation\n- Places: Netherlands, Hoogeveen\n\nFacts:\n- Jacobus Arminius, the Latinized name of the Dutch theologian Jakob Hermanszoon from the Protestant Reformation period, was born on October 10, 1560 and died on October 19, 1609.\n- Jacobus Arminius was a student of Theodore Beza, John Calvin's successor, at the Theological University of Geneva.\n- The Calvinist–Arminian debate began in the early 17th century in the Netherlands as a Christian theological dispute between the followers of John Calvin and Jacobus Arminius.\n- From 1603, Jacobus Arminius served as professor of theology at the University of Leiden.\n- The Calvinist–Arminian debate continues today among some Protestants, especially evangelicals.\n- Geert Booij, born in 1947 in Hoogeveen, is a Dutch linguist and emeritus professor of linguistics at the University of Leiden.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
